What data structures needed to be synchronized, and why?

The global tables map in Server need to be synchronized for each thread because they can all access it at the same time. 
Each Table object also needed to be synced as the key-value pairs that may be read or modified by multiple threads. To ensure consistency, a mutex is used for every table.
The transaction state for each thread is also synchronized, ensuring that each thread correctly locks/unlocks tables during transactions is essential for thread safety.

How did you synchronize the data structures requiring synchronization?

A pthread_mutex_t tables_mutex in Server guards all accesses to the tables map.
Each Table has its own mutex and the handlers call lock()/unlock()/trylock() for reads/writes.
During autocommit Tables are locked during each operation and unlocked afterward. WHile during transaction mode, Tables are locked with trylock() to avoid deadlocks, and all successful locks are tracked in m_locked_tables. When committed, all locked tables are then unlocked. When there is a failure, changes are discarded and locks are also released.
Transactional operations record and release table locks with m_locked_tables to ensure atomic multi‑table commits.

Why are you confident that the server is free of race conditions and deadlocks?

All mutexes follow a consistent acquisition order tables_mutex->Table locks preventing lock-order inversion.
Locks are always released in the same scope or during rollback/commit, ensuring no mutex remains held after an error.
Transactions acquire each table’s lock at most once and release all locks at commit or rollback to avoid cyclic waits.